WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.170
Does it ever seem like the wealthy have some
secret formula that’s hidden from the rest

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.170
of us?

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:11.929
Well, lean in close because the best kept
secret of the rich is a single word: Debt.

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:17.109
That’s right -- you don’t have to actually
own any money to make money, if you’re smart

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:18.109
about it.

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:19.109
Whoa there sparky!

00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:21.740
Listen to yourself -- debt as a gateway to
riches?

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.880
That’s like going for a swim with a lead-life-preserver!

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.700
Just look at all the businesses affected by
COVID19 and how much harder it was for those

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.249
with lots of debt.

00:00:31.249 --> 00:00:37.520
Oh, you’re talking about BAD Debt -- and
yeah, BAD debt is bad, it’s in the name.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.500
I’m talking about that sweet, “good”
flavor of debt that accelerates dreams and

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.500
opens doors.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.880
Wait...there’s “good debt” and “bad
debt” now?!

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.000
Oh, and of course there are the more bad “good
debts” and more good “bad debts”.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:07.510
Debt might seem like a relatively modern phenomenon,
with stories of national deficits and credit

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.080
card balances dominating the headlines.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.760
But debt is a very, very old human invention.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.840
Writing was invented in ancient Mesopotamia
in order to keep track of debts.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.880
In fact, debt even predates the invention
of money.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.010
Wrap your head around that!

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:30.650
In his book “Debt: The First 5,000 Years”,
David Graebner of the University of London

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:37.370
argues that it was debt, not physical money,
that made economies hum for thousands of years.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:42.760
Debt made civilization possible, really, by
allowing us to track who owes what to who

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.510
more easily than physical bartering.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:49.590
And the seemingly modern urge to run away
from our debts and live a simple life in the

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:54.250
wilderness might also be an instinct as old
as society itself.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:59.659
Graebner writes, "People would just start
running away, joining nomadic bands and periodically

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:01.990
kings would declare debt cancellations."

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:07.520
It’s really not that hard to see why we
have such a love-hate relationship when it

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.959
comes to debt.

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:13.620
It can help you achieve your goals much faster
-- like a small business loan to open up your

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.439
dream bakery, or a student loan to help you
pay for graduate school.

00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:22.920
But, when things get dicey, it has the annoying
tendency to make us become the star of our

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.200
own production of “Naked and Afraid.”

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.510
Conventional wisdom goes that good debts are
incurred for things that are expected to increase

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.590
in value over time - think a house or small
business.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.290
Bad debts don’t -- like a trip to Cancun
or a fancy dinner.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:45.099
While most of us may have a few “bad debts”,
experts generally agree that those debts limit

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:46.689
your ability to build wealth.

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:49.780
But from there, things get complicated.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:55.010
You might’ve heard media personalities take
strong stances on the use, purpose, and even

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.660
existence of “good debt.”

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:04.620
And two of the loudest modern voices in this
debate have been Robert Kiyosaki and Dave Ramsey.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.460
In his bestselling book “Rich Dad, Poor
Dad”, Kiyosaki claims to have learned critical

00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:14.249
financial lessons from a friend’s father,
aka his “Rich Dad”.

00:03:14.249 --> 00:03:19.579
Such lessons included the value of education,
the power in working for yourself, and the

00:03:19.579 --> 00:03:23.340
importance of always investing with Other
People’s Money.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.140
He writes,”Good debt helps you get rich,
Bad debt makes you poor: If you want to get

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.820
rich, load up with ‘good debt’.”

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.230
Good debt, in his view, is a way to build
something out of nothing, and it’s allowed

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.799
him to build a massive real-estate portfolio
and multiple businesses.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:45.249
Critics of Kiyosaki point out that he’s
“good-debted” himself into bankruptcy

00:03:45.249 --> 00:03:46.689
on a few occasions.

00:03:46.689 --> 00:03:52.290
And that it’s mighty convenient that he
sells real estate courses and speaks to multi-level-marketing

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:58.109
companies like Amway, both of which stand
to profit if audiences use debt to “buy

00:03:58.109 --> 00:03:59.790
in to the opportunity.”

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:05.150
In the other corner we have Dave Ramsey, who
preaches that a “good debt” is an oxymoron.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:11.020
Ramsey’s syndicated radio show opens with
the tag-line “Debt is dumb, and cash is king."

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:16.060
He advocates that having no debt of any kind
- including a mortgage - is a hallmark of

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.060
success.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.260
This makes sense in light of his personal
story.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:25.009
In his mid-20’s, Ramsey had built a multi-million
dollar real-estate portfolio, purchased with

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:26.270
mortgage debt.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:31.400
When the bank called all of his notes at once,
he went from millionaire to filing bankruptcy

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.900
fast enough to make your head spin.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.949
Now, he has a similar view of debt that someone
recovering from substance abuse might toward

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.330
drugs or alcohol.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.200
Ramsey has his share of critics, too.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.080
For starters, they point out that buying a
house without a mortgage is nearly impossible

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:48.280
for most people.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.150
Indeed, Ramsey has a mortgage company advertise
on his show!

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:57.479
Skeptics also argue that debt can be harmless
if used carefully.

00:04:57.479 --> 00:05:01.870
Like using a credit card to receive points
and paying off the balance in full.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:07.199
And for those with a low-paying job, trying
to survive an emergency without any debt often

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:08.639
isn’t realistic.

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.690
We’ve personally found ourselves on both
sides of the fence.

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:18.330
We used to have a pretty friendly relationship
with debt, both the “bad kind” -- financed

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.930
cars, credit cards with balances -- and the
“good kind”.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:27.259
After a few years, we decided to pay off all
our debt except our mortgage, and went almost

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:31.080
seven years without using a single credit
card.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.569
Recently, we’ve evolved to utilizing credit
cards again, while always paying the balance

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:38.659
off in full every month.

00:05:38.659 --> 00:05:43.580
But what we’ve learned in the past decade
of financial work is that answering this question

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.909
isn’t usually solved by running the numbers.

00:05:46.909 --> 00:05:52.360
How you decide to interact with debt mostly
depends on personal values and tolerance for

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.360
risk.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.979
We came up with our stance by answering two
questions.

00:05:55.979 --> 00:05:59.349
First: How does your spending change with
debt?

00:05:59.349 --> 00:06:04.780
We’ve found we tend to spend more on a payment
plan vs. paying for something in full.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:10.110
Indeed, research suggests that for most people,
it’s natural to let spending creep up a

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:14.319
bit if it’s purchased on debt instead of
in cash.

00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:19.729
To paraphrase Socrates, “Know thyself before
choosing the installment plan.”

00:06:19.729 --> 00:06:23.409
Second: Is the risk worth the reward?

00:06:23.409 --> 00:06:26.419
The benefits from using debt can be life changing.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.949
But debt always carries risks.

00:06:28.949 --> 00:06:33.419
When opening a small business years ago, we
considered taking out a small business loan.

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:38.810
But the risks seemed bigger than the possible
reward of fast-tracking our venture.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.689
We opted to grow slower, but with less risk.

00:06:41.689 --> 00:06:44.820
In the end, Debt isn’t “good” or “bad”.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.659
It carries risk, but it can open doors.

00:06:47.659 --> 00:06:52.590
The “right” approach toward debt is ultimately
personal and specific to your situation.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:58.720
But proceed carefully, remember there is no
free lunch, and take things slowly so you

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.420
can learn  as you go.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.140
And that’s our two cents!

